-84. A counter that pins receipts pins every receipt it reads, held ids included: a digest check that runs only for new ids lets a held id's receipt be swapped for a forgery, and every census or clause recomputed from that receipt then blesses the forgery. A predicate docstring that promises byte-equality is not the check; the code path is. Check: the counter's FORGED-PIN control (a pinned file replaced, its Gate sha256 and any census recomputed to match) must be rejected while the otherwise identical positive control is admitted — `python3 -I .ufo/waves/round-44/ctl/run-c2-controls-v2.py --repo . --freeze 03fa5c618b089064a657b3d79d3d4272ae4b6894 --base 6a1a90528fe533cadee506a9fa3e9d13d15cc41f --work <scratch>` prints `CTL_C2v2 FORGED-PIN … rejected … d:0`. Shown able to fail: under the v1 counter the same forgery was admitted (`.ufo/waves/round-44/w1/CritC2Cheat/frozen-F2-FORGED-PIN.txt`, `w1/RevC2Counter/log-forged_s164.txt`); the branch had been inherited by four C2 counters (r41-v2, r42, r43-v2, r44). (lesson: lessons/round-44.md M44-8)
